Confirmed: at `991e500^`, `init()` already received the Activity context but correctly narrowed it to `context.getApplicationContext()` before storing and registering the receiver, so no leak existed. The commit `991e500` removed the stored `Context` field and switched `registerReceiver()` to take the raw `context` parameter passed into `init()`, which is the `MainActivity` instance itself — this is what introduced the leaked `BroadcastReceiver` registered against an Activity context that is never unregistered.

## Bug-Introducing Commits
BIC: 991e500e28c9e21d28512418a0e0f4fae62560b9

## Reasoning

**(1) Investigation process.** The fix commit (`8eeef35`) adds `MainActivity.onDestroy()` → `DownloadManagerInterface.destroy(this)`, which calls `context.unregisterReceiver(downloadCompletionReceiver)`. This strongly suggests the bug is "a `BroadcastReceiver` is registered somewhere but never unregistered, leaking whatever `Context` it was registered with." I traced the history of `DownloadManagerInterface.java` (it was renamed from `data/DownloadManagerInterface.java` to `data/downloadmanager/DownloadManagerInterface.java`) and diffed each revision of `registerReceiver(...)` and `init(...)`.

**(2) The specific change.** Before `991e500`:
```java
private final Context context;       // stored at construction time
public static synchronized void init(Context context) {
    if (instance == null) {
